Restless legs syndrome is the indication for which pramipexole's dose–response, augmentation liability, and withdrawal profile are actually characterised — and it is the route by which most ME/CFS patients encounter the drug. The 2025 AASM guideline on RLS/PLMD treatment re-ordered the class hierarchy: iron repletion and alpha-2-delta ligands (gabapentin, pregabalin) are the centrepiece of standard care, and dopamine agonists are no longer preferred first-line for chronic RLS because of augmentation @Winkelman2025RLSguideline.

#clinical-finding(title: [Dopamine Agonists Are No Longer First-Line for Chronic RLS; Augmentation at RLS Doses Is Quantified])[
The 2025 AASM clinical practice guideline (GRADE-graded systematic review) establishes iron repletion plus alpha-2-delta ligands as the standard of care for RLS/PLMD and removes dopamine agonists from preferred first-line therapy for chronic RLS on account of augmentation @Winkelman2025RLSguideline. The GRADE grades: strong recommendations (moderate certainty of evidence) for gabapentin enacarbil, gabapentin, and pregabalin over no treatment; a conditional recommendation (moderate certainty) *against* the standard use of pramipexole, with a remark that it may be used in the short term when patients value symptom reduction over long-term adverse effects, particularly augmentation. The guideline's first step is addressing exacerbating factors (alcohol, caffeine, antihistaminergic, serotonergic, and antidopaminergic medications, and untreated obstructive sleep apnea). The dopamine-agonist efficacy itself is not in dispute — the re-ordering is augmentation-risk-driven. The quantitative basis: in the 52-week NEJM head-to-head (pregabalin 300 mg vs pramipexole 0.25 or 0.5 mg vs placebo, n = 719), augmentation over weeks 40–52 was lower with pregabalin than with pramipexole 0.5 mg (2.1% vs 7.7%, $p$ = 0.001) and numerically lower than with pramipexole 0.25 mg (2.1% vs 5.3%, $p$ = 0.08); 12-week efficacy favoured pregabalin over placebo (IRLS −4.5, $p$ \< 0.001) @Allen2014PregabalinPramipexole. A retrospective cohort of 231 idiopathic-RLS patients on pramipexole for more than one month found augmentation in 9.1%, with daily dose and treatment duration as the significant risk factors and an optimal dose cut-off of 0.375 mg/day (rising to 0.500 mg/day at ≥46 months' duration) @Takahashi2017RLSaugmentation.

Three consequences for an ME/CFS patient. First, the guideline-concordant sequence for RLS in ME/CFS is exacerbating-factor review, then iron assessment (ferritin + TSAT) and an alpha-2-delta ligand — not a dopamine agonist @Winkelman2025RLSguideline. Second, gabapentin/pregabalin are among the frequent ME/CFS co-prescriptions (pain, sleep, or RLS) and a given patient's chart may well already contain one, so "pramipexole plus gabapentinoid" co-prescription is often a class-substitution question rather than an additive one — the NEJM data say the alpha-2-delta ligand is effective vs placebo (IRLS −4.5 at 12 weeks) with a lower augmentation burden than pramipexole 0.5 mg; the trial contains no efficacy head-to-head between the two — the comparison it ran was the augmentation one @Allen2014PregabalinPramipexole. Third, where a dopamine agonist is still chosen, the quantified data support keeping the dose at or below 0.375 mg/day and treating any dose escalation as an augmentation-risk decision @Takahashi2017RLSaugmentation.

(Translation gap: RLS populations (adults, idiopathic/severe, Japanese cohort) → ME/CFS patients with RLS comorbidity. No RLS study stratified by or recruited ME/CFS patients; RLS-in-ME/CFS prevalence and phenotype (primary RLS vs secondary RLS-phenotype during neuroinflammatory episodes, see @sec:full-partial-agonist-d2-d3) may differ.)
(Certainty: 0.50. Evidence type: society guideline (GRADE systematic review) + 52-week multicentre RCT + retrospective cohort. Population: RLS (not ME/CFS). Replication: Allen 2014 and Takahashi 2017 augmentation rates are mutually consistent; guideline is current (2025). Severity applicability: unknown — the RLS cohorts were not stratified by ME/CFS severity; the severe-RLS subgroup in @Allen2014PregabalinPramipexole is the closest analogue.)

*Consequence:* An ME/CFS patient with RLS should expect iron and an alpha-2-delta ligand to be discussed before pramipexole; if pramipexole is used, the quantified risk of augmentation starts at 0.375 mg/day, and the presence of a gabapentinoid on the chart is a decision point (continue both, or substitute), not a footnote.
